workplan/2026-11-12-bidirectional-pipeline/OPEN-ITEMS-PLAN.md:126:| OI-57 | Defects the authors of T-061 to T-063, T-067 to T-072 and T-075 to T-085 found (2026-11-13), each with its home. (1) The gate boilerplate in every task file says only `research_tooling_separation` reads `origin/main`; T-075's evidence command (1) also finds the `attestation_*` checks (some blocking: read their `level:` in the registry) and `claims_docket`. The same boilerplate says each check prints `EXAMINED:`, which is false for `validate_cross_refs`, `doctrine_recheck`, `test_graph_audit`, `test_pipeline_contract`, `test_assess_cell_pilot`, `test_evidence_cell_state_2_3`, `test_validate_evidence_state_2_4` and `test_directness_2_2`: T-075. (2) `run_checks.py --selftest`'s INFO line says `--all` reports which checks print no count, and nothing does: T-075. (3) Selftests no registry entry runs (`column_vocabulary_audit`, `migration_reproducibility`, `readonly_db_open_audit`, `retired_vocabulary_audit`, `dbcore`, `emit_data_migration`, `migrate_db`, `emit_batch_sql`, `validate_verification_consistency`): T-075. (4) `migration_reproducibility` keys its rebuild cache on the migrations folder, not also on `scripts/migrate_db.py`: T-075. (5) Two stale known-debt suppressions, `lang-jur-map-deferred` and `c1-source-value-extractions-empty`: T-076. (6) `db._names_term` accepts an outdated adjudication (it passes if any adjudication of the observation names the term, not the latest; some observations carry more than one): T-074 (tooling, rule 10). (7) T-048 adds only the root registry's writer, and `root_id` is not an amendable figure column (`grep -n -A3 '_AMENDABLE_SVE_FIELDS = ' scripts/db.py`), so a registered root cannot be pointed at by an existing figure: **homed 2026-11-13 in T-048** (its section 4 item 4 adds `root_id` to the amendable set; the existence refusal is `dbcore.fk_declared` on the column's declared foreign key, so no new code), and T-071's precondition says so. (8) `v_value_independence` counts the roots of condition and finding rows (parameter 3 shows more roots over all rows than over value-supplying rows): an open design question reported by T-068. (9) T-025's per-pair approval is printed, not stored: a possible rule-8 defect, for the owner. (10) OR-15's prompt premise is false (batch 23 holds no T1 source): OD-44. (11) T-020's pilot patch predates the warrant column, so T-020's task file defines the column names, not the patch. (12) T-061's expected census line lists extractions 189 and 190, which T-082 will change: re-derive it on the day. (13) T-108's scope is narrower than the prior D-1's: it omits extractions 11 and 12 (a value reformat, executable with T-061) and the bare-`ratio` rows; its preconditions now name T-061, and the bare-`ratio` rows are T-086's (research, held on OD-41; carved out of T-108 on 2026-11-13 so that T-108 is not held). Extractions 11 and 12 are **homed 2026-11-13 in T-108** (its section 4 step 3 selects the `a-b` ranges by query and reformats them with T-061's verb; `grep -n -E 'extractions? 11\b|11 and 12' workplan/2026-11-12-bidirectional-pipeline/tasks/*.md` now finds it). (14) T-105's file omits the prior D-3's "re-grade `verified` only on a full scan (OR-11a)"; T-062 waits for that class to print 0 rows. (15) T-037 reads no `extraction_status`: OR-11a's "the renderer prints the status of each row" and OR-11b's "preliminary flagged" have no step. (16) T-030 needs: a derived `RULE_VERSION` bump; the done-when `dbcore.bound_on_scale('36','in','>=','numerical') == (Fraction(4572,5), None)`; "both notations are shown" defined as OR-10's prompt reads; the `NOTATION_FAMILIES` comment "composing across them stays refused" swept as a caller; whether `dbcore.unit_refusal`'s residual is closed; whether `specifications.value_notations` (a JSON array `joined_cells_census.py` reports) is extended or replaced. (17) `workplan/pipeline-state-graph/machines.yaml` M04.t2's amendable-field list lacks `jurisdiction`, which T-024 added: T-061 fixes it. (18) The fixtures of `scripts/tests/test_run_pipeline_refusals.py` (cases X02 and X03) take a fresh source id from the database's high-water mark, which is the id the next real admission takes (the Edmonton checklist digest of `session_2026-11-13-edmonton-checklist-pass`, whose retrieval manifest already holds it); with real saved text held for that id, `--verbatim-exempt` is refused and both cases go red. The fixtures should derive an id free in every home of an identifier, the retrieval log included (rule 8): T-073 (tooling; it isolates the fixture root from a pre-admission folder rather than changing the id rule, which the real admission needs), and the digest's batch should not ship before it. (19) The engine's "presumed echo" report line (prior T-8 step 3) was not made a task (nothing wrong reaches the guidebook without it; T-071 carries OR-19 as data): the owner confirms the decline or asks for the task. (20) The five live `condition_on` edges from a parameter-12 claim to a parameter-3 claim (OI-56) still need an owner. (21) E11 (`lang_jur_map.scope_status` is the bucket table OR-8 asked for) came through the blanket "apply all recommendations" reading, which that entry marks as an [ASSUMPTION]: ask the owner to confirm. (22) The age at which a standing deferral becomes a defect (T-079 builds the measure, no gate): a number only the owner sets. **Added 2026-11-13 by the fold of the Edmonton case study** (`scratchpad/session_2026-11-13-edmonton-checklist-pass/ADJUDICATION.md`; the critic's findings and the tool defects the adjudication did not give a proposal): (23) the engine's gap text for a pending cell states three things that are not so (`grep -n -F 'source(s)' scripts/assess/assess_cell.py`; `grep -n -F 'disqualified, or tier not derivable' scripts/assess/assess_cell.py`; `grep -n 'elif regulatory:' scripts/assess/assess_cell.py`): "from N source(s)" counts the gathered value-supplying sources, fewer than the sources in the cell's lens (ADJUDICATION F11; the third run's MOB cells, `DIGEST-REPORT.md` section 6, compare its in-lens query with the engine's `n_sources`); the cause "disqualified, or tier not derivable" is printed where every source record of the engine's `--report-json` reads `tier_consistent true`; and the regulatory branch writes `regulatory_stratum_only` and `code_floor_only` as 1 on a cell whose lens also holds a grey source. **Merged 2026-11-13** with the third run's and the second critic's finding of the same defect (F6). A tool defect, **owed** (no task; tooling, rule 10); T-087 explains each cell from `v_live_lens_links` and the engine's JSON meanwhile and lands the engine's text as written; the third run filed it as an SW gap on its scratch copy, which lands with T-087. (24) the lens-warrant writer accepts any words found in a file saved for the source, so a document's preamble can warrant every figure in it (R13; `grep -n -F 'named_in_text       the figure' scripts/db.py`): **homed in T-035** item 4 as a convention, no code (the judge is the gate and the warrant is auditable); a writer refusal waits for T-031's clause inventory, which can tell a preamble from an item. (25) `governance/adversarial-use-framework.md` says Universal Mode is "never *best practice*" (`grep -n 'never \*best practice\*' governance/adversarial-use-framework.md`), which the 2026-10-30 rulings supersede on contact (rule 0): a prose caller in a doctrine file, **owed** to the next doctrine task that opens that file (owner sign-off applies to it; ADJUDICATION R11). (26) the 2026-08-27 building-level ruling's ACTION names `room_items` as the junction to re-point, and that table no longer exists (`python3 -I -c "import sqlite3;print(sqlite3.connect('file:data/guidebook.db?mode=ro',uri=True).execute(\"select name from sqlite_master where sql like '%room%'\").fetchall())"`): read by T-091's design under OD-49; nothing else is owed. (27) T-110 should quote T-090's coverage read (1) before it writes the first live determinations (a step owed to T-110's file, not written by the fold, which may edit only the files its brief names). (28) parameters 5 and 9 have no accessibility direction (`select parameter_id from base_parameters where accessibility_direction is null`), and the Edmonton checklist states which way is better for both; whether a grey committee's statement may set a direction (`set-parameter-direction --ref-id`) is the judging session's call in T-087, named in its pull request (ADJUDICATION F3). (29) writer gaps the replay of the Edmonton digest met and filed as `SW` gaps on its scratch copy (they land with T-087; `scratchpad/session_2026-11-13-edmonton-checklist-pass/DIGEST-REPORT.md` section 8 since the third run, which met all five again and filed each as an SW gap, except `data_capture_status`, which its section 12 records as not moved for want of a verb): `log-search` demands a subject even for an owner-supplied admission where no search ran; `log-mining` cannot record a deferred forward direction once a backward pass has marked the source mined; no writer holds `evidence_sources.pub_year_note` (`add-source` has no flag, `amend-source` refuses it); `raise-follow-up` has no reason for an ambiguous printed figure; and no verb moves `data_capture_status`: each **owed** a tooling task (none authored by the fold, which had no evidence beyond the replay's sentence for any of them), and each is the kind of refusal T-088 keeps and T-089 classes. The replay also recorded the checklist's figures under `CA` because no Alberta or Edmonton code is held: T-113's subject (OD-01 delegated). **Added 2026-11-13 from the Edmonton digest's third run and the second critic** (`scratchpad/session_2026-11-13-edmonton-checklist-pass/DIGEST-REPORT.md` section 11; `REPLAY-V3-BRIEF.md` F2, F6, F8; (d) of that list is merged into (23), and its (h) adds nothing, all four writers being in (29)): (30) the research gate's R16 reads only adjudications that name a term (`a.term_id IS NOT NULL`) and reads all of them, not the latest (no `max(adjudication_id)` in its join), and no gate reads a `DEFERRED` adjudication (`grep -n 'undisposed = _rows' -A8 scripts/audit/research_batch_dod.py`; `git grep -n -E "'DEFERRED'" -- scripts governance/check-registry.yaml ':!scripts/migrations'`): so `DEFERRED` can hide a settled act and keep R16 green, and a later re-adjudication cannot clear an earlier one; T-079's adjudication arm makes standing `DEFERRED` rows visible with their age, but nothing gates them; a gate-design defect, **owed** (tooling, rule 10). (31) the research gate's R1 passes on the substring `CO1-NOT-APPLICABLE` in a search's `findings_note` without reading the reason (`grep -n "CO1-NOT-APPLICABLE%" scripts/audit/research_batch_dod.py`), so it passed the Edmonton batch on a waiver that concedes the topic's Co-1 pass was not done; unlike R5's waiver token, no reason after the token is required: **owed** (tooling). (32) the relation vocabulary of `extraction_relations` has no kind for a source's recommended figure beside the floor it states (`select sql from sqlite_master where name='extraction_relations'`: `exceeds` means the referent is more than needed, `delta_over` is an increment, `confirms` an independent measurement), and `extraction_relations_integrity` checks the shape of an edge, not its meaning, so an inverted `exceeds` passes CLEAN (`grep -n -E 'relation' scripts/audit/extraction_relations_integrity.py`); a writer or vocabulary question (a new CHECK value is a schema migration); the Edmonton batch parks its pairings meanwhile (`grep -c '"kind": "pairing"' scratchpad/session_2026-11-13-edmonton-checklist-pass/parked-writes.jsonl`): **owed** (tooling). (33) `evidence_population_match` can target an identity only (`target_population` is free text with no foreign key, `select sql from sqlite_master where name='evidence_population_match'`, but the writer refuses any value not in `base_taxonomy_identity`, `grep -n -F 'is not in `base_taxonomy_identity`' scripts/db.py`, and the engine conditions population directness on the identity lens only, `grep -n -F 'Only the' -A4 scripts/assess/assess_cell.py`), so a cell keyed on an access need or an ICF code always reads `population_directness NOT_ASSESSED` (the third run's need and ICF cells, `DIGEST-REPORT.md` section 6): **owed** (tooling; it bears on the four-lens ruling, so whichever task re-keys the match table to the lens, none today). (34) `term_adjudications.term_id` references `terms` only (`select sql from sqlite_master where name='term_adjudications'`), so a group phrase ("persons who are blind", "users who are Deaf or hard of hearing") cannot be adjudicated to its identity code and can only be `DEFERRED`, with a rationale that says it records a withheld act: **owed** (a schema or writer question). (35) `add-gap` has no `--slug` flag and `gaps` has no slug column (`python3 scripts/db.py add-gap --help`; `python3 -I -c "import sqlite3;print([r[1] for r in sqlite3.connect('file:data/guidebook.db?mode=ro',uri=True).execute('pragma table_info(gaps)')])"`), so a gap about a slug's research pass (the Edmonton topic's Co-1 pass) carries the slug only in its text: **owed** (a writer question; whether a gap should be scoped to a slug at all, since gaps are keyed on parameter and lens, is part of it). (36) **Divergence diagnostics reader owed: the section "What the investigation looks at" in `tasks/T-068-convergent-counts-roots.md` is its specification** (owner statements 2026-11-14: `grep -n -F "Owner statement 2026-11-14 — convergence means values coming together" references/project-standards.md`). For each divergent parameter (T-068's investigation queue: `v_divergence` and the cluster cue gaps) and each pair of its clusters, a read-only comparison of the typed attributes that differ, from rows already held: the lens links (`v_live_lens_links`); condition edges and `condition` rows; `setting`, then T-091's typed circumstance; `device_class`, `measurement_paradigm`, jurisdiction, publication year, tier and root; a stepped series from one source; a trigger comparator; building use (residential against non-residential, public, commercial: T-091's carrier, pointed at); design mode (`specifications.design_scale` against the extraction's lens links: one source targeting everyone and another a named group is a reason to read the clusters apart, not a defect); a held interval spanning two clusters (`dbcore.bound_on_scale`; a mirror 1000 to 1200 mm above finished floor spanning points at 1000 and 1200); and, where nothing differs, "undetermined" with the claim texts, context quotes and modifier words printed for a human read. The list is a starting list the reader prints, not a closed vocabulary (the owner's "etc"). **Rule-8 justification:** every check reads an existing column or relation, so nothing is curated beside the data; the one list is the modifier-word screen, which only chooses words to highlight in text printed whole (a missing word hides nothing), lives in the reader alone, and is maintained by the reader's owner. **Open question for the task's author:** is a view or a script the smaller code (a view serves the per-attribute differences in SQL but cannot run `bound_on_scale` or print the modifier screen; a script can, and adds a file): **owed** (a tooling task, after T-068 and T-091). Evidence for each is in the task file named, by command | the task files of T-061 to T-085; `REMEDIATION-WORKPLAN.md` §8 (OD-40 to OD-46) | each item's home has absorbed it (a task file corrected, a task authored, or an owner line); items (6) and (18) each have a tooling task (T-074, T-073), and the owner items are OI-58's | B | Opus-class coordinator for the task-file corrections (11) to (17); T-074 carries (6) and T-073 carries (18), T-073 before the Edmonton batch ships (T-087 depends on it); T-048 carries (7) and T-108 extractions 11 and 12 of (13) since 2026-11-13; T-035 carries (24); T-087's judging session carries (28); still owed: (23), (25), (27), (29), (30) to (36); T-086 carries (13)'s bare-`ratio` rows; T-075 and T-076 carry (1) to (5); the owner items (9), (19), (20), (21), (22) are OI-58 |
workplan/2026-11-12-bidirectional-pipeline/OPEN-ITEMS-PLAN.md:129:| OI-60 | **RESOLVED 2026-11-14 (later): OD-56 ANSWERED BY THE OWNER, "b"** (ledger: `grep -n -F "Owner answer 2026-11-14 — OD-56" references/project-standards.md`; the reading as option (b) is an [ASSUMPTION], told to the owner and not corrected): an element-level determination lists the provisions an element should carry, by pointer; a provision is not a parameter and no value is computed for it. No delegated resolution or check is needed; options (a), (c) and (d) are not taken. **The work is T-060** (the element determination also points at the provision terms and the source statements that say the element should carry them; still no value column) **and T-029** (the `element has provision` pointer kind, with a test); T-072 item 6 and T-087 carry a one-line note. The row as registered follows. **OD-56**, registered 2026-11-14 on the owner's answer 4 of the thirteenth set ("hearing loops and other similar provisions should be included"; ledger: `grep -n -F "Owner answers 2026-11-14 (thirteenth set)" references/project-standards.md`): how a provision whose question is whether it is present (a hearing loop, a visual alarm, a tactile walking surface indicator, a TTY, tactile signage) is included in the book with a determination or mark of its own. The owner ruled inclusion; the mechanism is open, with four options in the register entry (a quantity kind of presence; an element-level list under T-060; a typed register of provisions; prose only, which the owner's answer rejects). Meanwhile no research batch declines such a term (OD-53's scope note). The same set answered OD-43 (any two kinds of evidence; most common practice, not best practice) and OD-44 (A), and narrowed OD-49 to building use and interior or exterior | `REMEDIATION-WORKPLAN.md` §8, `### OD-56`; `DECISION-CLOSURE.md` §4 prompt 18 | a delegated Opus-class resolution of OD-56 under the owner's twelfth instruction as applied at the owner's question of 2026-11-13, recorded in the ledger with `OD-56 STATUS: RESOLVED UNDER DELEGATION` and confirmed by an independent Opus-class check (`OD-56 CHECK:`), then the task the chosen option names, authored apart (rule 10) | B | Opus-class resolver, then an independent Opus-class checker, then an Opus-class author of the task |
workplan/2026-11-12-bidirectional-pipeline/OWNER-SHEET-PLAIN.md:15:**You answered:** "yes convergence is possible too, and it helps define most common practice (which isn't same as best practice)". **What was done:** agreement between any two kinds of evidence now counts, as disagreement does, still only when the sources give the same value or range, can all hold, and rest on at least two independent sources (two sources that repeat one study count as one). Agreement is shown as the most common practice, never as best practice: best practice stays the most accommodating value found. The decision on agreement (OD-43) is now yours, and the plan's task for it was rewritten to match.
workplan/2026-11-12-bidirectional-pipeline/OWNER-SHEET-PLAIN.md:72:So today a value for wheelchair users that rests only on codes would be labelled "universal", as if it were for everyone. The July rule's purpose was to stop a value resting only on codes from ever being shown as best practice. Under A that protection is kept by the separate codes-only mark, which already exists.
workplan/2026-11-12-bidirectional-pipeline/OWNER-SHEET-PLAIN.md:129:- **OD-43:** settled by you on 2026-11-14 (the first of the four things above): any two kinds of evidence may agree, resting on at least two independent sources, and agreement is most common practice, not best practice. The assistant's earlier decision, which allowed agreement only between controlled research and lived-experience evidence, is replaced.
